# Trimming the build context is mostly about layer-cache stability: any file in
# here that changes invalidates the COPY layers even when the build never reads
# it.
#
# Deliberately NOT excluded: internal/router/dashboard.html, which go:embed
# compiles into the binary, and internal/router/benchdata, which ships with it.
# Excluding either produces a build that succeeds on a developer's host and
# fails only inside the image.

.git
.gitignore
.github

# owned by other tooling; no build step reads them
deploy/
docs/
PLAN.md
README.md

# runtime concerns. A copy baked into the image would only ever go stale, and
# .env in particular would bake secrets into a published layer.
.env
.env.example
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# `go build ./...` ignores _test.go entirely, so tests are pure context weight.
# Note the consequence: this image cannot run `go test` — the CI workflow tests
# on the runner, not in the builder stage.
#
# The **/ prefix is required, not decoration: dockerignore matching is
# filepath.Match, where * does not cross a path separator, so a bare *_test.go
# would only ever match the context root and quietly ship every test under
# internal/.
**/*_test.go

# compiled output, mirroring .gitignore. A host-built artefact copied into the
# context can shadow or bloat the in-image build.
discrimen
**/*.exe
**/*.exe~
**/*.dll
**/*.so
**/*.dylib
**/*.test
**/*.out
**/coverage.*
**/*.coverprofile
**/profile.cov
go.work
go.work.sum
